WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.970
Hi, welcome back to Arya Nagarjuna.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.012
We're still in the middle of Chapter 10,

00:00:08.012 --> 00:00:12.022
which is "An Examination
of Fire and Firewood".

00:00:12.022 --> 00:00:14.725
And this was supposed to clear it up,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.270
rather the seer is there before the seeing

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.100
and especially before the thing you see,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.100
or is the seer come later, or
do they come at the same time?

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:32.810
Is it even possible that
we are seeing things

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.120
the way we think we are?

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.160
If you are not seeing things

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.580
the way you think you're seeing things,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.070
then sooner or later you get in trouble,

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.760
you start fighting with your
husband, or you do things

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.830
that don't make sense
to make things happen.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.720
Like, I don't know, in your
business, you take actions

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.230
which don't result in the
result you think they do

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.763
because you don't understand this question

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.810
of is the seer seeing something
before the thing is there,

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:05.770
or is the thing there first,

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.380
and then the person sees it later?

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.783
Of course, it applies to Medicine Buddha.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.410
If you're doing a mantra,

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.707
what's the time frame
between you and the mantra?

00:01:16.707 --> 00:01:20.190
Do you come first and
then you do the mantra?

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.830
But how can you come first if
you're a mantra practitioner

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:26.910
because you haven't done a mantra yet?

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.260
So actually, the mantra should come first,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.200
and then the person who
says it should come later,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.150
but then a mantra can't say
itself, and it gets confusing.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:39.760
If it gets confusing to you,
you are on the right track.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.130
If it's not confusing,
you're on the wrong track.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.370
If it seems confusing to you,

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.630
then it's shaking up your
normal way of seeing things,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.700
which according to
Buddhism is always wrong.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.257
You have always been wrong.

00:01:54.257 --> 00:01:58.342
Your opinion about whether
the mantra came first

00:01:58.342 --> 00:02:01.545
or the mantra sayer came
first, or how the mantra works.

00:02:01.545 --> 00:02:05.618
If you think you know, you're
probably making a mistake.

00:02:05.618 --> 00:02:08.321
And then when you say the
mantras, they're not as effective.

00:02:08.321 --> 00:02:09.595
It's not as powerful.

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:10.995
It's much more powerful

00:02:10.995 --> 00:02:13.028
if you understand how it really works.

00:02:13.028 --> 00:02:15.270
And it's like the relationship

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.170
between wood and fire, firewood and fire.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.500
So we're halfway through our examination

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.650
of firewood and fire. Here we go.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:28.910
We're going to go to verse
number eight, and it says,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.493
,GAL TE SHING LTOS ME YIN LA,

00:02:30.493 --> 00:02:32.285
,GAL TE ME LTOS SHING YIN NA,

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.251
,GANG LTOS ME DANG SHING 'GYUR BA,

00:02:34.251 --> 00:02:36.466
,DANG POR GRUB PA GANG ZHIG YIN,

00:02:36.466 --> 00:02:39.943
This is a question from Arya Nagarjuna.

00:02:39.943 --> 00:02:43.190
GAL TE SHING LTOS ME YIN LA

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.956
Is it the case that we have a fire

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:47.822
which is depending on the firewood?

00:02:47.822 --> 00:02:50.381
Or GAL TE ME LTOS SHING YIN NA

00:02:50.381 --> 00:02:53.264
is it a case that we have wood

00:02:53.264 --> 00:02:55.005
which depends on its identity,

00:02:55.005 --> 00:02:59.238
firewood, which depends
on its identity for fire?

00:02:59.238 --> 00:03:01.554
So the fire would have to come first

00:03:01.554 --> 00:03:04.820
because it can't be
firewood if it came first

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.088
before the fire.

00:03:06.088 --> 00:03:07.815
You have to have a fire.

00:03:07.815 --> 00:03:10.270
Then you say, oh, this wood is firewood.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.870
But if you don't have the wood first,

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.800
before it's firewood,
how can you have a fire?

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.280
And how can you have a fire
from wood which is not firewood?

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.155
Something's fishy.

00:03:21.155 --> 00:03:23.376
Something's wrong.

00:03:23.376 --> 00:03:25.762
GANG LTOS ME DANG SHING 'GYUR BA

00:03:25.762 --> 00:03:27.917
And somebody says,

00:03:27.917 --> 00:03:29.940
"I think they kind of
depend on each other."

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.760
The fire needs the wood to start burning,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.150
but the wood needs the fire

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.450
before you can call it firewood.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.560
So somebody says, "Geshe-la, I think

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.300
it's kind of like they're
depending on each other."

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.120
DANG POR GRUB PA GANG ZHIG YIN

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.810
But then we have to still have a question

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:55.920
of which came first,
the chicken or the egg?

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.760
Which came first, the
firewood or the fire?

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.332
DANG POR GRUB PA GANG ZHIG YIN

00:04:00.332 --> 00:04:03.030
means which one came first?

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.880
Why? Why is that important?

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.240
Now, listen and think about it.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.070
Because the second thing to come

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.620
always depends on the first thing to come.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.250
The second thing that comes always depends

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.370
on the first thing that comes.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.520
Curing cancer with a mantra come second,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.023
and that depends on the
mantra coming first, right?

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.070
But if it's that the mantra
depends on the curing,

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.600
in other words, if it doesn't cure cancer,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.042
then it's not really magical words, is it?

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:37.690
It's not really a mantra.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.950
So in that case, first
you should cure somebody

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.860
with some kind of words
and then those words

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.440
would become a mantra.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.140
But then how do you know what words to say

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.860
if you don't know if they're a mantra yet?

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.940
You could just read the side
of a cereal box or something,

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.840
and see if it helps.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.000
So it doesn't get to be a
mantra until you cure somebody,

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.980
but then if it doesn't get to be a mantra

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.520
until you cure somebody,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.590
you'd have to keep saying different words

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:06.432
until something worked, and
then that would be a mantra.

00:05:06.432 --> 00:05:08.450
You see what I mean?

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:12.860
So don't forget that
the second thing to come

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.190
has to depend on the first thing to come.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:20.190
Is it that the cancer is
cured rests on the mantra,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.070
or is it that it's the
mantra rests on the cure

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.090
because the mantra is not special words

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.703
until there's something happens from it.

00:05:27.703 --> 00:05:29.960
GAL TE SHING LTOS ME YIN NA

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.500
If it's the fire that came first,

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.015
and then that depends on the wood,

00:05:36.015 --> 00:05:38.160
ME GRUB PA LA SGRUB PAR 'GYUR

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.487
Then fire could've happened with no wood.

00:05:41.487 --> 00:05:45.030
If the fire comes first,

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.900
and then the wood becomes firewood,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.640
well, then the fire
doesn't depend on the wood

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.373
because the thing it depends
on has to come first, right?

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:59.700
If the fire comes first and
then it depends on the wood,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.520
then in the very beginning of the fire

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.220
there could be fire with no wood,

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:07.183
before it was depending on the wood.

00:06:07.183 --> 00:06:08.763
BUD PAR BYA BA'I SHING LA YANG

00:06:08.763 --> 00:06:12.903
If the wood came first... by
the way, we're on Verse 9,

00:06:12.903 --> 00:06:14.881
ME MED PAR NI 'GYUR PA YIN

00:06:14.881 --> 00:06:18.820
then without a fire the
wood would be firewood.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.080
Before there was any fire,
the wood would be firewood.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.900
Before you saw a fire come from it,

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.380
you could call it firewood.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:33.000
All right, and we have a
picture here of firewood.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:33.833
And a...

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.151
the idea is, I mean,

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.983
it's not firewood yet
until there's a fire.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.480
It looks like somebody tied it up.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.830
It looks like somebody
cut it in a certain way.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.400
We don't know for sure yet
though that it's firewood.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.090
We don't know if someone's
not using these sticks

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:55.810
for something else.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:59.400
So until there's a fire,
this is not firewood,

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.120
and until there's
firewood, there's no fire.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.630
So maybe things don't
happen the way we thought.

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:09.810
Maybe the mantra doesn't fix the cancer.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:14.090
Maybe something else is creating
the mantra and the cancer,

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:17.000
and the fix of the cancer.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.752
Like maybe our seeds,
maybe our mental seeds.

00:07:19.752 --> 00:07:22.348
Then, well, how do you use that, Geshe-la?

00:07:22.348 --> 00:07:24.810
How do you apply that to your life?

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:28.900
Well, then it makes sense
to save animals' lives

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.533
because then the mantra would work against

00:07:31.533 --> 00:07:33.093
the cancer, for example.

00:07:33.093 --> 00:07:35.540
There would be seeds in your mind

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:39.410
for a cause and effect
relationship between the mantra

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:42.077
and the curing the cancer.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.370
I did a three-year retreat,

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.245
2000 to 2003.

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:54.220
And I remember we had
a lady in the retreat.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.140
She wasn't from our lineage.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.727
She was from a different
lineage but she begged me,

00:07:59.727 --> 00:08:02.100
"Could I set up a cabin?

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:03.950
Could I do this 3-year retreat?"

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.170
We had, I don't know, five, six people

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:09.047
in different cabins, yurts. And she said,

00:08:09.047 --> 00:08:11.554
"Can I do it with you guys?"

00:08:11.554 --> 00:08:13.820
And we said okay.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:17.320
And she got permission from her teacher.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.067
And then I remember...

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:22.510
before the retreat and after the retreat,

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:25.618
we couldn't talk during the retreat, but

00:08:28.099 --> 00:08:31.500
we used to have very intense discussions,

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.720
and it was a very interesting question.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:40.010
She would say, "The power of your teacher

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:42.450
does not come from you."

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.770
Everything else maybe comes from you.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:46.670
Everything else comes from your seeds,

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:50.650
but the power of your teacher
is just in your teacher,

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.610
and that doesn't come from you.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.010
Whether you're a good
person or a bad person,

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.390
it doesn't matter.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.650
What seeds you have doesn't matter.

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:04.210
If a person is your
teacher, then there's power.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:05.520
The person has power.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.060
Then we'd say, "Well,
yeah, but Buddha said it."

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:10.993
The first lines of this book,

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.087
Buddha said, "Everything comes from causes

00:09:14.087 --> 00:09:15.890
and everything comes from seeds."

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:20.375
That's the first lines of
Nagarjuna's most famous book.

00:09:20.375 --> 00:09:22.283
GANG GIS RTEN CING 'BREL BAR 'BYUNG

00:09:22.283 --> 00:09:25.150
'GAG PA MED PA SKYE MED PA

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:27.440
Those are the first words:

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:29.795
everything comes from seeds.

00:09:32.937 --> 00:09:35.420
So it's not that a mantra has power.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.950
It's not that a mantra has power

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.060
even if it has been spoken by a Buddha.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.690
If you don't have the
seeds for it to have power,

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.140
it doesn't have power.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.296
Or else the Buddha was wrong.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.780
Or else the person you say has the power

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.703
didn't have the power.

00:09:52.703 --> 00:09:54.183
Okay, got it?

00:09:56.382 --> 00:09:59.843
If the Buddha has power in
and of himself or herself,

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.870
then everything they say is true.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:06.650
If the Buddha said all
things come from seeds

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:10.429
and even the power of the
Buddha comes from seeds.

00:10:10.429 --> 00:10:12.120
Okay, got it?

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.090
And it can't be wrong because
the Buddha that you say

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:17.332
doesn't come from seeds, said it.

00:10:17.332 --> 00:10:18.711
Got it?

00:10:18.711 --> 00:10:20.456
All right.

00:10:20.456 --> 00:10:21.922
All right, here we go.

00:10:21.922 --> 00:10:23.780
That's very Nagarjuna, right?

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:24.903
All right.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.402
Next verse.

00:10:27.402 --> 00:10:29.676
,GAL TE DNGOS PO GANG LTOS 'GRUB,

00:10:29.676 --> 00:10:31.702
,DE NYID LA YANG LTOS NAS NI,

00:10:31.702 --> 00:10:33.826
,LTOS BYA GANG YIN DE 'GRUB NA,

00:10:33.826 --> 00:10:36.810
,GANG LA LTOS NAS GANG ZHIG 'GRUB,

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.516
If the fire depends on the firewood,

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:42.562
then the fire...

00:10:46.425 --> 00:10:49.340
If the wood came first
and the fire came second,

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:52.662
then LTOS BYA GANG YIN DE 'GRUB NA

00:10:52.662 --> 00:10:55.610
if the thing being
depended upon by the fire

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:57.566
which is the firewood,

00:10:57.566 --> 00:11:00.670
GANG LA LTOS NAS GANG ZHIG 'GRUB

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:05.670
what did the firewood depend
on to be called firewood?

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:09.210
So, okay, I understand the
dependence goes one way.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.740
If I say is there a dependent origination?

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.640
Is there a dependent
relationship with fire?

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:16.870
Then you say yeah.

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:17.990
- Well, what?

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:20.130
- Well, the fire depends on the firewood.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:23.110
You can't have a fire without some fuel.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.800
- Yeah, but on the other hand,

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:27.970
the wood depends on the fire.

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:29.052
That's what it says here.

00:11:29.052 --> 00:11:33.379
LTOS BYA means the wood that
the fire is depending upon.

00:11:33.379 --> 00:11:34.515
GANG YIN DE 'GRUB NA

00:11:34.515 --> 00:11:36.643
As soon as you have the firewood,

00:11:36.643 --> 00:11:38.780
GANG LA LTOS NAS GANG ZHIG 'GRUB

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.480
well what did it depend
on to be called firewood?

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:43.600
That's a question.

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:48.487
I like to call it a ladder with no wall.

00:11:48.487 --> 00:11:50.826
Here's a picture of it.

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:53.602
It took me a while.

00:11:53.602 --> 00:11:55.740
I was trying to make
a ladder with no wall.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:58.310
It's almost impossible,

00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.840
but

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.320
anyway, it didn't come out that great.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.320
But try to imagine a ladder
that's leaning on nothing.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.440
Then that's what it would
be if the fire was firewood

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.120
before the fire starting,
then you could have a ladder.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.810
You could get up on a ladder

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.430
that wasn't leaning on a wall.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:25.170
That leaning on a wall is dependence,

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.710
and that's dependent origination.

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:30.610
And we're going to talk a lot
about the words for dependence

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.360
and what dependence means.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:35.930
Does the curing cancer
depend on the mantra

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.901
or does the mantra depend
on curing the cancer?

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:41.783
Or do they both depend on something else?

00:12:41.783 --> 00:12:43.850
That's what we would say.

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:46.320
And that's what Nagarjuna would say.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:50.080
There's a reason why we
took our bodhichitta vows

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.702
from whose ceremony?

00:12:52.702 --> 00:12:55.730
[Student] Nagarjuna.
[GMR] Nagarjuna, okay.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:59.020
We took our vows, which
is the permission we need

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:00.880
to study Medicine Buddha.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:04.550
We have to take vows and
the most famous ceremony

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:05.730
is Nagarjuna.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.760
So maybe Nagarjuna knows
something about Medicine Buddha.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.283
Okay, all right.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:13.973
Here we go. Verse 11.

00:13:15.752 --> 00:13:18.202
,DNGOS PO LTOS GRUB GANG YIN PA,

00:13:18.202 --> 00:13:20.052
,DE MA GRUB NA JI LTAR LTOS,

00:13:20.052 --> 00:13:22.526
,CI STE GRUB PA LTOS SHE NA,

00:13:22.526 --> 00:13:24.852
,DE NI LTOS PAR MI RUNG NGO,

00:13:24.852 --> 00:13:27.543
DNGOS PO LTOS GRUB GANG YIN PA

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:32.530
There's a very important
word here called LTOS GRUB,

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:36.110
and this is interesting for us.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:40.700
It means things happen
because of other things.

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:44.450
And the most famous, I
think, Sugeng's text,

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:46.330
the Mixed Nuts' text.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:51.200
We are translating a very
difficult commentary by Vasubandhu

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.060
on the Wheel of Life.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:55.693
And you will see there

00:13:55.693 --> 00:13:58.980
the definition of dependent origination.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:02.171
The definition of dependence,

00:14:02.171 --> 00:14:03.980
pratītya samutpāda (pratitya samutpada),

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:05.873
is LTOS GRUB.

00:14:05.873 --> 00:14:08.240
LTOS GRUB, here's the word LTOS GRUB.

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:09.763
LTOS GRUB means

00:14:09.763 --> 00:14:14.763
everything that changes happens
based on something else.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:18.890
Everything that changes
in the universe happens

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:20.552
depending on something else.

00:14:20.552 --> 00:14:22.620
'DI 'BYUNG BAS NA DE 'BYUNG

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:25.260
And the very, very
earliest statement of it

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.107
is by Lord Buddha, and he said,

00:14:27.107 --> 00:14:30.870
"If this happens, that will happen.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:34.160
And if that happens, this must happen."

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.907
And he's like this, you can see him.

00:14:37.907 --> 00:14:41.340
"If this happens, then this will happen."

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.137
It's like your mom used to tell you,

00:14:43.137 --> 00:14:46.540
"If you do this, this is going to happen,

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.920
and if this happens, is
because you did this."

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.150
Then I don't know, after
that it got complicated.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.860
People started calling
it dependent origination.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:58.292
In the beginning, it was very simple.

00:14:58.292 --> 00:15:01.690
If you do this, guys,
you're going to get this.

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:03.531
That's LTOS GRUB.

00:15:04.864 --> 00:15:07.413
DNGOS PO LTOS GRUB GANG YIN PA

00:15:07.413 --> 00:15:09.900
DE MA GRUB NA JI LTAR LTOS

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.060
How could fire depend on wood?

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:17.280
And now we get into a
tricky thing with Nagarjuna.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.400
And it seems a little

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.380
unfair.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:26.000
If you back him into a corner,

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.814
if you say, "Yeah, but
you can't have a fire

00:15:27.814 --> 00:15:29.840
without a firewood."

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:33.029
And he's, "Okay, okay,

00:15:33.029 --> 00:15:38.029
but the fire you think you
can't get without firewood,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:41.430
it doesn't exist anyway."

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:42.960
And you're like, "Wait, wait.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:46.462
Now, you're changing things."

00:15:46.462 --> 00:15:47.404
And he says,

00:15:47.404 --> 00:15:50.160
"Yeah, well, don't tell me
the fire depends on the wood

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.147
because the fire can't exist anyway."

00:15:53.147 --> 00:15:54.604
- Well, what do you mean?

00:15:54.604 --> 00:15:57.567
- The fire you're thinking of can't exist.

00:15:57.567 --> 00:15:58.427
- Why?

00:15:58.427 --> 00:16:00.307
- Because you think it comes from wood.

00:16:00.307 --> 00:16:01.987
- But I'm trying to
prove it comes from wood.

00:16:01.987 --> 00:16:05.147
- I know, but that means you
think it comes from wood.

00:16:05.147 --> 00:16:05.980
- Yeah, I think it comes from wood,

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:07.557
that's what I'm trying to prove to you.

00:16:07.557 --> 00:16:09.260
- But the fire you think comes from wood,

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.300
can't come from wood,

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.380
so you can't prove the
fire comes from wood

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.270
because the fire you want
to prove comes from wood

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.528
doesn't exist.

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.471
And it's like you're
frustrated, you're like,

00:16:20.471 --> 00:16:22.650
"Man, I was trying to
prove something to you."

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:26.111
It's like having a
discussion with your wife.

00:16:26.111 --> 00:16:27.244
DNGOS PO LTOS GRUB GANG YIN PA,

00:16:27.244 --> 00:16:28.902
DE MA GRUB NA JI LTAR LTOS

00:16:28.902 --> 00:16:31.557
CI STE GRUB PA LTOS SHE NA

00:16:31.557 --> 00:16:34.520
Then the other guy, it's funny.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.033
CI STE means well, what the hell!

00:16:37.033 --> 00:16:38.872
The guy said, "I don't care."

00:16:38.872 --> 00:16:41.870
CI STE is a very short word, right?

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:42.703
CI STE

00:16:42.703 --> 00:16:45.670
But it means ah, anyway.

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:47.860
The guy's got CI STE, CI STE.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:51.057
Anyway, the fire doesn't
depend on the wood.

00:16:51.057 --> 00:16:52.780
DE NI LTOS PAR MI RUNG NGO

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:57.780
And then Nagarjuna says,
"No, you can't have a ladder

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:03.423
leaning on a wall that's not there."

00:17:03.423 --> 00:17:08.050
You can't have a ladder
leaning or depending on a wall

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:10.240
if the wall's not there.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:12.830
- What's not there?
- There's no firewood there.

00:17:12.830 --> 00:17:13.663
- Why?

00:17:13.663 --> 00:17:17.420
- There's no fire yet,
how can it be firewood?

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.710
Maybe it's, I don't know,
wood they're going to use

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:23.573
to build a cafe when they
get a permit in three years.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:25.960
Something like that.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:29.150
You don't know what the wood is yet.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:30.500
All right.

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:36.320
And then I like this picture,
and this is an old picture

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:37.990
that we use in DCI.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:41.463
It's a pen, parts of a pen on a table.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:44.940
I hope so.

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:53.280
The point here is that the
resting of the pen on the desk

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.763
is also not self existing.

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:02.900
We went through many
times why it's not a pen

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:04.810
until the seed opens in your mind.

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:07.870
We talked about that countless times.

00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:12.750
But now if you look at the
pen resting on the table,

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.670
the pen resting on the desk,

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:16.260
that's also coming from your seeds.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:21.260
The dependence of the pen on
the parts sitting on the desk

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.790
is also coming from your seed.

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:26.173
Okay, all right, next verse.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:35.313
Now some guy says,

00:18:36.987 --> 00:18:38.700
"I have an option."

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:39.983
- What?

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.850
- The fire depends on the wood

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.202
and it doesn't depend on
the wood at the same time.

00:18:45.202 --> 00:18:46.770
How's that?

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:48.380
He's getting frustrated and saying

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:50.630
the fire doesn't depend on the wood

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:52.470
because it's not a firewood yet,

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:55.230
and the firewood needs
the fire before it can...

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:58.950
He says, "Look, Ok, Nagarjuna,
I have a suggestion.

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:00.907
Let's do both at the same time."

00:19:00.907 --> 00:19:04.260
The fire depends on the wood

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.500
and it doesn't depend on
the wood at the same time.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:07.610
How's that?

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:10.819
Then you're happy and I'm happy."

00:19:10.819 --> 00:19:14.102
,SHING LA LTOS PA'I ME MED DE,

00:19:14.102 --> 00:19:16.680
,SHING LA MA LTOS ME YANG MED,

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:20.460
There's no fire that could depend on wood,

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:24.080
and there's no fire that
couldn't depend on the wood.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:27.171
Therefore, ME LA LTOS PA'I SHING MED DE

00:19:27.171 --> 00:19:30.103
There's no wood that
could depend on the fire,

00:19:30.103 --> 00:19:33.115
and there's no wood that
couldn't depend on the fire.

00:19:33.115 --> 00:19:36.027
There's no fire that
doesn't depend on wood,

00:19:36.027 --> 00:19:38.614
but there's no fire that
does depend on wood.

00:19:38.614 --> 00:19:41.845
There's no wood that depends
on the fire to be firewood,

00:19:41.845 --> 00:19:45.150
and there's no wood that
doesn't depend on the fire

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:45.983
to be firewood.

00:19:45.983 --> 00:19:49.060
How's that? Let's leave it like that.

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:51.030
The other guys said,
"You can have your cake

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:52.000
and eat it too."

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:56.460
And Nagarjuna's answer is, ridiculous.

00:19:56.460 --> 00:20:00.914
And I try to make a ridiculous picture.

00:20:00.914 --> 00:20:03.570
Pachi, are you ready? I don't know.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:06.122
I hope it's ridiculous.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.196
It's a cup on a table.

00:20:11.373 --> 00:20:13.100
I don't know.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:15.750
I tried really hard on this one.

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:20.670
The cup is on the table
but it's not on the table

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.900
because the table is on
the cup at the same time.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:26.570
The cup is on the table and
the table is on the cup.

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:30.480
So Nagarjuna, you don't
have to struggle deciding

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:33.960
whether the fire rests on the wood

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.670
or the wood rests on the fire.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:37.292
How about both?

00:20:38.138 --> 00:20:39.178
Let's do both.

00:20:39.178 --> 00:20:42.620
Unfortunately, the cup
would only be one, right?

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:46.260
And then it'd be difficult
to make a photo of it.

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:48.680
Almost impossible, I think so.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.870
All right, let's go to the next verse.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:56.090
He splits the next verse into two.

00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:58.010
And when he does that,

00:20:58.010 --> 00:20:59.980
there's always an
important reason for that.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:04.013
So we're going to take
half a verse which is 13a.

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:08.964
And it's very simple.

00:21:08.964 --> 00:21:12.581
,ME NI GZHAN LAS MI 'ONG STE,

00:21:12.581 --> 00:21:16.993
,SHING LA'ANG ME NI YOD MA YIN,

00:21:16.993 --> 00:21:20.051
And what this means is the other guy says,

00:21:22.347 --> 00:21:24.600
"You can see it. If you can see it,

00:21:25.728 --> 00:21:30.370
If you can see fire burns wood,

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:35.370
then fire does have a
nature, and it burns things."

00:21:35.388 --> 00:21:38.510
Don't tell me nothing has its own nature.

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:41.225
You don't have to be a philosopher

00:21:41.225 --> 00:21:43.374
to see that things are not empty.

00:21:43.374 --> 00:21:45.660
They are something, they're not empty.

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.790
Fire is not empty. - Why?

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:50.533
- You can see it burns wood.

00:21:50.533 --> 00:21:52.970
If a fire burns something,

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.380
then you can't say it has
no nature. It has a nature.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:56.438
What's its nature?

00:21:56.438 --> 00:21:58.967
It burns things, duh, you can see.

00:21:58.967 --> 00:22:02.070
Stop hassling me, that's all.

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:05.240
The other guy's like, "Open
your eyes, Nagarjuna, look."

00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:07.570
And probably he'd lit
a match or something.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:10.870
He said, "Look, the fire is
burning the wood. Okay, got it?"

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:13.170
Don't tell me it doesn't
have a nature of its own.

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:16.690
Don't say the match fire is empty

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:20.110
because it's not empty
because it's doing something.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:23.170
If you can see that it's doing something,

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:24.740
it's not empty.

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:27.070
Don't give me this empty stuff.

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:29.967
So that's a very, very...

00:22:29.967 --> 00:22:30.800
In Tibetan it's

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:33.058
MES BUD SHING SREG PA MNGON SUM GYIS

00:22:33.058 --> 00:22:36.680
MTHONG BAS NGO BOS YOD DO ZHE NA

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:41.450
The other guy says, Look, if
a fire burns a piece of wood,

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:42.877
then MNGON SUM GYIS MTHONG BAS

00:22:42.877 --> 00:22:45.230
if you can see it with your own eyeballs,

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:47.120
then the fire has its own nature,

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:51.013
and it's not empty of
any nature of its own.

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:56.640
Now, we're going to get
to Nagarjuna's answer.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:58.593
But first of all,

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:01.060
he says,

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:03.427
and there's an American
English expression:

00:23:03.427 --> 00:23:06.362
Seeing is believing. Seeing is believing."

00:23:06.362 --> 00:23:08.770
The other guy says, "Look,
seeing is believing."

00:23:08.770 --> 00:23:11.810
And Nagarjuna says, "Let
me show you a picture now."

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:13.780
Let me show you a picture with somebody

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:16.290
with certain glasses on.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.740
And there's an expression
in American English called

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:23.690
"looking at things with
the rose colored glasses."

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:27.930
Actually I gave a talk once long ago

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.750
and I bought 100 pairs of these glasses.

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:33.280
I handed them out to everybody,

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:35.210
and I said, "Is the world pink?"

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.735
And they're all like, "Yes."

00:23:37.735 --> 00:23:41.040
And I, "Yeah, I can see it's pink."

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:44.960
So it must be. You said
if you can see something

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.800
is a certain way, it must be that way.

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:52.140
A guy says, "Yeah, okay.
Well, put on these glasses.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:53.177
Is everything pink?"

00:23:53.177 --> 00:23:54.010
- Yeah.

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:55.760
- Is it really that way?

00:23:56.999 --> 00:23:57.897
- No.

00:23:57.897 --> 00:23:59.637
- Yes, it is, because you're seeing it.

00:23:59.637 --> 00:24:01.087
Seeing is believing.

00:24:01.087 --> 00:24:03.890
If you see everything
in the universe is pink,

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:05.063
then it must be pink.

00:24:05.063 --> 00:24:06.200
That was your argument.

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:08.470
Then the guy says, "No, no,

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:10.150
you've you messed up my eyes.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.287
You put these pink sunglasses on me."

00:24:12.287 --> 00:24:14.790
- Yeah, well, maybe
without the sunglasses,

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:16.778
your eyes are messed up.

00:24:16.778 --> 00:24:18.207
All right?

00:24:18.207 --> 00:24:21.390
Maybe you're messing up
everything all the time anyway.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:22.790
And that's the idea.

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:27.270
Okay, so the Wheel of Life teaching

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:31.450
is basically that your
equipment is messed up.

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:35.767
Your eyes are already
tinted. And you can say,

00:24:35.767 --> 00:24:39.780
"Fire must depend on wood
because any fool can see it."

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:42.240
- Yeah, but if the way you look at things,

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:44.907
you're always a fool. All right?

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:51.158
Okay, here we go to the
next half verse again.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:54.683
,DE BZHIN SHING GI LHAG MA NI,

00:24:54.683 --> 00:24:57.574
,SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM PAS BSTAN,

00:24:57.574 --> 00:25:00.115
,DE BZHIN SHING GI LHAG MA NI,

00:25:00.115 --> 00:25:02.090
,SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM PAS BSTAN,

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:05.000
Now, Nagarjuna, he goes
an interesting strategy,

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.380
and by the way, in this whole book

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:11.430
and most of Nagarjuna's books
are like a military campaign.

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:15.780
I saw a movie about Alexander
the Great. He's so cool.

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:20.650
I mean, he was a brutal killer
but his first great battle,

00:25:20.650 --> 00:25:24.340
I think he's got 40,000 Macedonians

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:28.793
against a million Persians. And he's 21.

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:34.440
And it's 20-1, and he goes out there,

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:37.587
and he's like, "Okay, I got an idea."

00:25:37.587 --> 00:25:42.540
And his strategy is so cool.
He rides out with 100 guys,

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:45.220
and they take off that
way so that the soldiers

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:47.090
are facing the soldiers, right?

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:48.680
He comes out with like 100 guys,

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:51.210
and he runs that way with his horses.

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:54.757
And the other general's
like, "Where's he gone?"

00:25:54.757 --> 00:25:56.137
- I don't know.

00:25:56.137 --> 00:25:58.990
- We're supposed to go like
this and fight with each other.

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:00.297
- What's he doing?

00:26:00.297 --> 00:26:01.757
- He's gone somewhere.

00:26:01.757 --> 00:26:04.850
- But you, you, you, you, follow him.

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:05.930
Find out where he's going.

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:07.210
And then he circled around.

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:10.830
The general was no longer
protected, the Persian general,

00:26:10.830 --> 00:26:12.580
and he...
[shooting sound]

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:14.100
Okay, got it?

00:26:14.100 --> 00:26:16.257
Now, why are we talking about that?

00:26:16.257 --> 00:26:17.870
Nagarjuna does the same thing.

00:26:17.870 --> 00:26:19.850
He sees this whole thing

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:22.360
almost like a military campaign.

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:25.103
And here's his next move.

00:26:25.103 --> 00:26:26.459
Here's his next move.

00:26:26.459 --> 00:26:27.989
DE BZHIN SHING GI LHAG MA NI

00:26:27.989 --> 00:26:30.530
LHAG MA means leftover, right, Tim?

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:32.980
LHAG MA means leftover.

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:35.240
He says, "Look, let's just stop there

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:36.647
about the fire and the firewood."

00:26:36.647 --> 00:26:39.840
It's been going on too long.
It's already two classes.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:41.930
Let's just finish it.

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:44.460
He says, "Anything else you wanted to say

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:46.590
about the firewood, just stop."

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:48.840
And the guy said "Whoa, wait."

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:51.890
He says, "Go back to
SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM.

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:55.830
Go back to the chapter
about walking down a path,

00:26:55.830 --> 00:26:58.133
which is the second chapter.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:00.930
"Going and Coming"

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:01.780
He says, "Look,

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:04.410
I don't have to do this
whole argument with you."

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:05.800
I'm tired of this chapter.

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:07.610
we got to go on to the next chapter.

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:10.070
It's already too much time.

00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:12.210
Let's get onto the next chapter.

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:16.400
If you want to talk more about
anything else about firewood,

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.767
can you just go back and
read the second chapter.

00:27:18.767 --> 00:27:21.700
Again, I told you everything.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:23.830
What did he tell us in the second chapter?

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:25.250
Anybody?

00:27:25.250 --> 00:27:28.642
Remember something about
there's three parts to the path.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:32.660
Three parts to a road.

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:34.310
Do you remember?

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:41.000
There's the part you already walked on,

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:44.240
then there's the part
you're going to walk on,

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:46.700
and then there's the part
you're walking on now.

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:49.080
Now, how wide is the part
you're walking on now

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:51.560
is the question.

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:54.240
Okay, like your foot goes like,

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:58.580
here's the path and your
foot heels touches first,

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:01.560
and then your toes take off, right?

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:04.670
Heel, foot, toe.

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:08.220
This part is the path you already went on.

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:10.520
This part is the path you didn't go on.

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:14.680
Now, exactly where's the
line between those two?

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:16.840
- Well, it must be halfway.

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:19.077
- Well, let's wait until
you put your foot down.

00:28:19.077 --> 00:28:23.197
- Okay, and you want to
call it halfway the foot?

00:28:23.197 --> 00:28:24.217
- Yeah, yeah, okay.

00:28:24.217 --> 00:28:26.460
- Well, where's the
halfway point on your foot?

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:27.642
You can't find it.

00:28:27.642 --> 00:28:29.835
You can't really find it.

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:34.053
Any line you draw, if
you can see the line,

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:39.710
it already has width.
So it already has a part

00:28:39.710 --> 00:28:41.513
that you went on already and a part

00:28:41.513 --> 00:28:43.720
that you didn't go on already.

00:28:43.720 --> 00:28:45.140
- Okay, we'll split that one, Geshe-la.

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:46.810
- Okay, let's split that one.

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:48.733
And then that has a
width, and that has a part

00:28:48.733 --> 00:28:50.940
that you went on and a
part that you didn't go on.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:52.580
And the answer is there's no part

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:54.582
that you're walking on now.

00:28:54.582 --> 00:28:58.740
You always either walked on something

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:00.950
or going to walk on something,

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:03.070
but you're never walking on something.

00:29:03.070 --> 00:29:04.980
So if you're not walking on something,

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:06.990
how can you ever have
walked somewhere before

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:09.800
and how can you ever go
into walk on something?

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:11.200
Because you're not walking.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.940
- Geshe-la, it's all bullshit.
You're just splitting hairs.

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:16.490
- Yeah, but no, it's important.

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:18.170
You think you're walking but you're not.

00:29:18.170 --> 00:29:20.495
Maybe the path is moving.

00:29:22.598 --> 00:29:23.840
Okay?

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:25.070
Maybe your mental pictures

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:27.150
are getting bigger,
bigger, bigger, bigger,

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:29.230
as you go through a door,

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:32.470
bigger, bigger, bigger door,
smaller, smaller, smaller door.

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:34.530
You call it walking to a door.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:36.560
But maybe you're not walking to a door.

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:38.857
Maybe your mind is making
small door, big door, big door,

00:29:38.857 --> 00:29:40.030
big door, big door,

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:41.595
little, little door, little door,

00:29:41.595 --> 00:29:42.590
little, little, little door.

00:29:42.590 --> 00:29:47.470
Maybe you're standing in
the same place all the time.

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:48.540
Something like that.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:51.650
Geshe-la, what's the point of all that?

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:54.510
Well, then it means
you're creating the door,

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:56.380
and you're creating the
passage to the door,

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:59.647
and that means you can
go anywhere you want.

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:05.317
If it's coming from here,
then you could walk planets.

00:30:05.317 --> 00:30:08.210
You could act out your bodhichitta.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:12.180
That's how you're going to be a Buddha.

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:13.973
- Oh, if you say Medicine Buddha mantra,

00:30:13.973 --> 00:30:15.393
you become Medicine Buddha.

00:30:15.393 --> 00:30:17.430
- Yeah, what's that all about?

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:19.180
- Well, then you can
go to different planets

00:30:19.180 --> 00:30:21.230
and help people at the same time.

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:22.420
- Why?

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:24.310
Because the planet gets
bigger, bigger, bigger

00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:25.870
when you arrive and the planet

00:30:25.870 --> 00:30:26.950
gets smaller, smaller, smaller

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:28.650
when you go to the next planet.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:30.230
That's all coming from your mind.

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:32.660
Hey, why not just do it
all at the same time?

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:33.980
And then you're everywhere.

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:36.450
And you can help everybody
at the same time.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:37.620
That's all.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:39.320
If the travel to other planets

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:42.440
was coming from anywhere
else except your seeds,

00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:44.320
it's impossible.

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:46.220
You can't. - Why?

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:48.905
- There's no place you are now.

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:52.250
Okay, got it?

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:53.566
It's very sexy.

00:30:53.566 --> 00:30:57.260
Because of emptiness you
can become Medicine Buddha.

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:58.770
And if you don't understand that,

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:02.932
you will never become
Medicine Buddha. Got it?

00:31:02.932 --> 00:31:05.915
So he says, "Go back to
what I told you before."

00:31:05.915 --> 00:31:09.440
Now, Grandpa likes to
take you back to the...

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:11.240
I could just go onto the next verse,

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:15.330
but why don't I take you
back to the last verse.

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:18.887
And we don't have it on the thing.

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:23.490
Anyway, I went back and got
the whole second chapter.

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:26.450
It's all about that walking thing.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:29.460
Nagarjuna, you can feel
like he's almost impatient.

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:33.470
Now he wants to get onto
his eleventh chapter, right?

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:36.207
He says, "Just remember what I told you

00:31:36.207 --> 00:31:39.060
about the walking thing."

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:41.310
By the way, every text I
saw or many of the texts,

00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:43.635
they misspelled the walking thing.

00:31:43.635 --> 00:31:46.030
And if you spell it wrong,

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:50.250
it becomes meditation.
And then it gets messy.

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:53.973
So it's useful to have the Sanskrit.

00:31:53.973 --> 00:31:55.121
All right.

00:32:01.468 --> 00:32:04.731
Which means, I don't
know, I was in a mood,

00:32:04.731 --> 00:32:07.614
the next picture we're going to look at.

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:15.240
To me, the most important
application of fire

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:19.190
is making a pizza in a firebrick oven.

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:21.940
Do we have any around here? I don't know.

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:22.773
Yeah?

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:27.250
So if you can't decide,

00:32:27.250 --> 00:32:28.680
is the fire burning the pizza

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:32.000
or is the pizza causing the
fire to be called the fire

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:35.120
because it gets cooked by the thing?

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:36.502
I think

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:42.180
the discussion of whether
the pizza defines the fire

00:32:42.180 --> 00:32:45.333
or the fire cooks the pizza

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:49.197
what's it similar to?

00:32:49.197 --> 00:32:51.870
What did Nagarjuna just say?

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:53.007
What did he just say?

00:32:53.007 --> 00:32:54.519
You can't find.

00:32:57.455 --> 00:32:58.288
[TL] You can't find the now.

00:32:58.288 --> 00:33:00.200
[GMR] Yeah, you can't find the now,

00:33:00.200 --> 00:33:02.762
therefore you cannot bake a pizza.

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:06.650
And that's why they're parallel.

00:33:06.650 --> 00:33:08.840
That's why he wants to
bring the second chapter

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:10.300
into the tenth chapter.

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:15.300
You can't find a place
where the pizza is cooking.

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:19.870
In time, temporarily,
you cannot find a place

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:22.290
where the pizza is done.

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:24.403
It doesn't exist.

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:28.027
He's just sitting in front
of the guy and he's like

00:33:28.027 --> 00:33:30.333
I'm so tired of this fire.

00:33:30.333 --> 00:33:33.043
Agni - Indana

00:33:33.043 --> 00:33:36.343
The guy says, "Any last
words you want to give me?"

00:33:42.536 --> 00:33:43.692
- You can't have a pizza.

00:33:43.692 --> 00:33:44.525
- Why?

00:33:44.525 --> 00:33:47.197
- There's no moment when it's done.

00:33:47.197 --> 00:33:48.701
Got it?

00:33:48.701 --> 00:33:49.887
Because the pizza doesn't depend on fire,

00:33:49.887 --> 00:33:52.797
and the fire is not a pizza
fire until the pizza is done.

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.290
Got it?

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:59.300
I don't know why I decided to use a pizza.

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:02.196
Hey, Jigme, let's buy a pizza.

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:05.883
Okay.

00:34:08.470 --> 00:34:11.620
I'll just tell you the
English of the old verse.

00:34:11.620 --> 00:34:13.870
That part I've already translated.

00:34:14.777 --> 00:34:17.560
"First of all, having gone is not going,

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:20.210
neither is not having gone going,

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:23.190
and yet, except for having
gone and not having gone,

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:25.425
you can never be stepping."

00:34:25.425 --> 00:34:30.425
That's part of the lines
that he's talking about.

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:34.690
All right.

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:37.830
Oh, and then there's another
verse later on that's relevant,

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:41.640
and he says, "Fire as an
example to establish seeing

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:45.890
is not something which can
having gone or not gone,

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:49.640
or stepping down, offers
a reply to the one

00:34:49.640 --> 00:34:52.060
who is tied to seeing."

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:55.730
In other words, if you
always believe your eyes,

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:59.390
then I'll give you the
argument about walking.

00:34:59.390 --> 00:35:00.970
You cannot always believe your eyes.

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:01.803
- Why?

00:35:01.803 --> 00:35:04.720
- I told you about the walking thing.

00:35:04.720 --> 00:35:06.540
It looks like there's a
place where you're walking,

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:07.850
but there's not.

00:35:07.850 --> 00:35:09.770
So don't trust your eyes.

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:11.440
That's the subject we're on right now.

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:15.040
Don't tell me the fire
must depend on the wood

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:18.420
because you can see it burns
when there's wood there.

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:20.640
Then we say, don't trust your eyes.

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:22.617
You also think you're walking, don't you?

00:35:22.617 --> 00:35:26.350
You also think you're walking
to a door, but you never are.

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:29.121
- But my eyes tell me so.
- Yeah, that's the problem.

00:35:29.121 --> 00:35:31.128
[laughing]

00:35:31.128 --> 00:35:32.486
All right.

00:35:33.470 --> 00:35:35.430
We can go to verse 14.

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:37.840
This is a long run, I'm sorry.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:42.507
I think Nagarjuna also wants
to get onto the next verse.

00:35:45.529 --> 00:35:50.100
Nagarjuna here, he's
kind of making a review,

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:53.436
and he says, "Let's look at
the four possibilities here."

00:35:53.436 --> 00:35:55.286
,SHING NYID ME NI MA YIN TE,

00:35:55.286 --> 00:35:57.319
,SHING LAS GZHAN LA ME YANG MED,

00:35:57.319 --> 00:35:59.310
,ME NI SHING DANG LDAN MA YIN,

00:35:59.310 --> 00:36:01.844
,ME LA SHING MED DER DE MED,

00:36:03.370 --> 00:36:04.877
Here's the four possibilities.

00:36:04.877 --> 00:36:07.820
So Nagarjuna is kind of summarizing.

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:11.180
- Look, wood is not fire.

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.090
Wood is not the fire.

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:14.580
- Why?

00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:17.613
And by the way, I want
you to think about this

00:36:17.613 --> 00:36:20.019
because the way Choney Lama does it

00:36:20.019 --> 00:36:21.472
is a little bit tricky.

00:36:21.472 --> 00:36:23.060
You're ready?

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:25.470
Wood is not the fire

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:28.753
because in that case, the
agent would be the action.

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:36.681
That makes wood the agent.

00:36:39.323 --> 00:36:43.557
So it's not that fire's burning wood,

00:36:43.557 --> 00:36:46.035
it's that wood is feeding a fire.

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:48.103
Okay?

00:36:48.103 --> 00:36:50.540
Who came first, the chicken or the egg?

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:53.980
Is the fire burning the wood?

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:57.150
Is that what this chapter is all about?

00:36:57.150 --> 00:36:59.440
And Choney Lama says, "Oh no, no.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:01.840
The wood is feeding the fire."

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:04.357
The fire is the object of the action,

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:08.300
not the agent.

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:10.750
The wood is feeding the fire.

00:37:10.750 --> 00:37:12.550
Then the guy says,
"Well, I thought the fire

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:13.970
was burning the wood.

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:15.511
I thought the fire was the agent

00:37:15.511 --> 00:37:17.050
and the wood was the object."

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:20.000
Then Nagarjuna says,
"Well, think about it.

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:22.542
You also thought you were walking."

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:25.070
I'll say it again.

00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:26.640
By the way, I'm sorry,
we have to go so fast.

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:29.760
We're trying to get through
this book in five years,

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:31.540
and this is the only way.

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:35.850
Right now, today, we
are moving at one wisdom

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:37.585
per five years.

00:37:38.473 --> 00:37:41.430
If we move slower, it's
going to be 20 years.

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:42.670
Really.

00:37:42.670 --> 00:37:44.076
Okay, here we go.

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:48.050
You're meant to look at these videos again

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:50.937
and again, and again until you get it.

00:37:50.937 --> 00:37:52.163
All right.

00:37:52.163 --> 00:37:55.203
Wood is not the fire because then

00:37:55.203 --> 00:37:57.360
the agent would be the action.

00:37:57.360 --> 00:38:00.733
When you say it that way, who's the agent?

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:02.690
I'll say it again.

00:38:02.690 --> 00:38:06.570
Wood is not the fire because
if the wood was the fire,

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:09.523
the agent would be the action.

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:15.670
He's saying the fire is
the object of the wood.

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:18.080
The wood is feeding the fire.

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:21.910
That kind of implies the wood came first

00:38:21.910 --> 00:38:23.687
and then it's feeding the fire.

00:38:23.687 --> 00:38:24.520
And he says,

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:28.194
"I don't want you to say the
fire is burning the wood."

00:38:28.194 --> 00:38:32.730
Then the fire came first and
the wood came second, right?

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:36.720
Sorry, yeah, the fire came
first and the wood came second.

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:38.078
All right.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:41.420
So are they the same thing?

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:43.560
Is the fire the wood? - No.

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:46.050
I mean, either the fire
is burning the wood

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:48.330
or the wood is feeding the fire.

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:52.160
But they can't really be
happening at the same time.

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:56.963
Then the second one is, is the
wood separate from the fire?

00:38:57.930 --> 00:39:00.847
And he says, "No, it's not."

00:39:00.847 --> 00:39:01.737
- Why?

00:39:01.737 --> 00:39:04.810
- Because when you say
separate from the fire,

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:08.320
you think they never overlap.

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:11.520
When you say Rebecca is
separate from Michael,

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:14.863
then what you mean is they're
not Siamese twins somewhere.

00:39:16.350 --> 00:39:18.248
She's over there. I'm over here.

00:39:18.248 --> 00:39:22.900
So there must be some line between them.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:25.600
That means they don't overlap.

00:39:25.600 --> 00:39:26.630
They can't overlap.

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:29.240
- Well, okay, if they never overlap,

00:39:29.240 --> 00:39:32.050
then they can't have a relationship.

00:39:32.050 --> 00:39:33.080
Not possible.

00:39:33.080 --> 00:39:36.630
If the fire never touches the wood,

00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:38.640
there can't be burning.

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:40.640
But if it does touch the wood,

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:44.017
then there's a place where
the wood is the fire.

00:39:44.017 --> 00:39:45.950
- No, no, no, the wood is over there,

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:48.277
the fire is over there,
Geshe-la. And they touch.

00:39:48.277 --> 00:39:50.625
- Yeah, but where do they touch?

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:55.470
- There's a spot where
they touch each other,

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:58.450
so they have to overlap
in a tiny space there.

00:39:58.450 --> 00:40:01.840
If they don't, then there's
always a line between them.

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.700
Then no one can burn the other

00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:05.360
because it never touches the other.

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:06.193
If it does touch the other,

00:40:06.193 --> 00:40:10.919
then there must be some
kind of overlap there.

00:40:10.919 --> 00:40:14.098
If the wood is not separate from the fire,

00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:18.110
you couldn't have the
fire burning the wood

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:20.573
because the fire is the wood.

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:26.500
All right. Does the fire involve the wood?

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:27.333
- No.

00:40:28.400 --> 00:40:32.330
- Is the fire completely devoid of wood?

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:34.350
- No, because then it wouldn't burn.

00:40:34.350 --> 00:40:37.640
So these are the four choices.
I'll say them again.

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:39.930
And again, you don't have to
understand it the first time,

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:42.360
just keep listening to it.

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:43.747
If you think you understood Nagarjuna

00:40:43.747 --> 00:40:46.086
the first time you read it,

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:50.560
you must be drunk or something.

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:51.393
All right.

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:54.316
Wood is not the fire

00:40:54.316 --> 00:40:58.241
because then the agent of the
action would be the action.

00:40:59.148 --> 00:41:00.620
Wood is not separate from the fire

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:01.900
because if they were separate,

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:03.923
then there couldn't be a fire.

00:41:06.250 --> 00:41:10.760
Fire doesn't involve
wood because if it did,

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:13.940
some part of the fire would be the wood.

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:16.057
And if you say that's
ridiculous, then I say,

00:41:16.057 --> 00:41:20.270
well, okay, then water can cause fire.

00:41:20.270 --> 00:41:21.103
- Why?

00:41:21.103 --> 00:41:22.855
- Because they don't touch.

00:41:23.690 --> 00:41:26.950
If fire were devoid of wood,
there would be no fire.

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:29.370
If fire had no relationship to wood,

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:31.600
how could you have a fire?

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:36.440
What's really happening
when fire burns wood?

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:39.498
You have the karma to
have a fire. That's all.

00:41:39.498 --> 00:41:41.070
You have the seeds to have a fire.

00:41:41.070 --> 00:41:43.150
The wood is not causing the fire.

00:41:43.150 --> 00:41:46.267
The wood is not feeding the fire,

00:41:46.267 --> 00:41:48.280
and the fire's not burning the wood.

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:50.870
Your seeds are opening.
That's all. That's all.

00:41:50.870 --> 00:41:52.930
That's what Nagarjuna wants you to...

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:56.210
That's all he wants you to understand.

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:58.447
The fire is not burning the wood

00:41:58.447 --> 00:42:01.100
and the wood is not feeding the fire.

00:42:01.100 --> 00:42:05.210
Your seeds are opening and you see a fire.

00:42:05.210 --> 00:42:10.030
Stop thinking that the
wood causes the fire

00:42:10.030 --> 00:42:12.820
or stop thinking that the
fire is burning the wood.

00:42:12.820 --> 00:42:14.210
Stop thinking like that.

00:42:14.210 --> 00:42:16.040
My seeds are opening into wood.

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:18.200
My seeds are opening into fire.

00:42:18.200 --> 00:42:19.033
That's all.

00:42:19.033 --> 00:42:20.720
Start trying to think like that.

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:23.480
My seeds are opening into
Medicine Buddha mantra.

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:27.190
And then my seeds are opening
into the cancer is stopping.

00:42:27.190 --> 00:42:29.630
My seeds put them together.

00:42:29.630 --> 00:42:32.170
My seeds make them cause and effect.

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:34.020
They are not cause and effect.

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:35.310
They can't be cause and effect

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:37.900
because one can't touch the other.

00:42:37.900 --> 00:42:40.730
No more than

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:45.730
where you went and where you're
going to go can never touch.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:47.750
[laughing]
Okay.

00:42:47.750 --> 00:42:48.653
All right.

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:53.990
Let's go to the next verse.

00:42:53.990 --> 00:42:56.527
I'm sorry, this one's really long.

00:42:56.527 --> 00:42:58.927
,MA DANG SHING GIS BDAG DANG NI,

00:42:58.927 --> 00:43:00.401
,NYE BAR LEN PA'I RIM PA KUN,

00:43:00.401 --> 00:43:02.567
,BUM RNAM SOGS DANG LHAN CIG TU,

00:43:02.567 --> 00:43:05.210
,MA LUS PAR NI RNAM PAR BSHAD

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:08.150
This is a pretty profound verse.

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:11.890
He says, look, we spent all
these two classes talking

00:43:11.890 --> 00:43:14.153
about wood and fire.

00:43:15.161 --> 00:43:16.984
NYE BAR LEN PA'I RIM PA

00:43:16.984 --> 00:43:21.401
I want you to apply that to
all actions by all people.

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:26.720
For example, to what we had
in the previous chapters:

00:43:26.720 --> 00:43:31.200
seeing, hearing, smelling,
tasting, feeling.

00:43:31.200 --> 00:43:34.060
There's an agent which is your eyeball.

00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:36.370
There's a blue color out there.

00:43:36.370 --> 00:43:39.403
The agent acts towards the object.

00:43:40.317 --> 00:43:42.740
Try: when you're struggling
with understanding

00:43:42.740 --> 00:43:44.480
how you can see something,

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:47.650
when you're struggling with
understanding how mantra works,

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:49.370
please just go back to
the fire and the wood.

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:51.410
That's what he's saying.

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:53.700
He says, "Use it for everything."

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:57.195
Then he says BUM RNAM. BUM PA, Tim knows.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:01.618
Coffee cup.

00:44:01.618 --> 00:44:05.820
And SNAM BU means a bolt of cloth.

00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:08.740
He's just taking two things in the room.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:11.157
The eyeglasses and the coffee.

00:44:11.157 --> 00:44:12.890
The eyeglasses and the coffee.

00:44:12.890 --> 00:44:17.360
What I said about the
fire and the firewood,

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:21.923
what I said about the eye,
eyeball and the blue color -

00:44:23.041 --> 00:44:25.450
Look, he's saying it's
the same with everything.

00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:30.210
Eyeglasses, coffee cups,
vases, bolts of cloth.

00:44:30.210 --> 00:44:32.235
It's the same with everything.

00:44:32.235 --> 00:44:34.210
MA LUS PAR NI RNAM PAR BSHAD

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:36.840
You can explain every god
damn thing in the world

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:40.820
if you just get this fire
and firewood thing straight.

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:41.653
What?

00:44:42.577 --> 00:44:44.427
The fire is not burning the firewood

00:44:44.427 --> 00:44:46.910
and the firewood is not feeding the fire.

00:44:46.910 --> 00:44:50.283
If you can get it, okay! then
you understand everything.

00:44:50.283 --> 00:44:52.630
Apply it to everything.

00:44:52.630 --> 00:44:55.620
I have a picture here.

00:44:55.620 --> 00:44:59.200
I was trying to think of
two things in the world.

00:44:59.200 --> 00:45:00.117
Two things in the world.

00:45:00.117 --> 00:45:02.980
I don't know, they should
be a refrigerator and a car.

00:45:02.980 --> 00:45:04.120
Maybe I messed it up.

00:45:04.120 --> 00:45:05.580
Oh, there you go.

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:08.743
Yeah, you can apply it to
everything: refrigerators, cars,

00:45:10.504 --> 00:45:12.210
bolts of cloth.

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.670
In the old days, they didn't
have cars and refrigerators,

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:17.800
so he had to say bolts of cloth and water.

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:19.340
Water vase is a popular example

00:45:19.340 --> 00:45:24.090
because there was no plumbing
in any of the houses.

00:45:24.090 --> 00:45:25.280
You had to have a vase.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:28.898
So it's a good example
because it's always there.

00:45:28.898 --> 00:45:30.970
You always have one.

00:45:30.970 --> 00:45:33.913
If you've ever lived
in a village in India,

00:45:34.890 --> 00:45:36.563
the 'kailash',

00:45:37.733 --> 00:45:39.541
the water vase or pot is

00:45:39.541 --> 00:45:41.840
a very important thing in your life.

00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:44.060
That's all your water for the day.

00:45:44.060 --> 00:45:45.557
You walk a mile to the well,

00:45:45.557 --> 00:45:47.367
you get it, and you put it on your head.

00:45:47.367 --> 00:45:50.050
You bring it home and
that's what you wash from,

00:45:50.050 --> 00:45:51.810
that's what you cook from.

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:53.800
That's what you... Yeah.

00:45:53.800 --> 00:45:56.590
Okay, I always got another verse here,

00:45:56.590 --> 00:45:58.604
I don't know what happened.

00:45:58.604 --> 00:46:00.438
,GANG DAG BDAG DANG DNGOS PO RNAMS,

00:46:00.438 --> 00:46:02.601
,DE BCAS NYID DANG THA DAD PAR,

00:46:02.601 --> 00:46:03.718
,RTOG PA DE DAG

00:46:03.718 --> 00:46:07.193
Oh, this is the final verse,
I think, and it's very funny.

00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:10.050
Yeah, it's the final verse.

00:46:10.050 --> 00:46:11.690
Very cute.

00:46:11.690 --> 00:46:13.840
He says, look:

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:15.640
,GANG DAG BDAG DANG DNGOS PO RNAMS,

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.467
,DE BCAS NYID DANG THA DAD PAR,

00:46:17.467 --> 00:46:22.050
If you think the fire and
the wood are the same thing,

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:26.313
or if you think the fire
and the wood are separate,

00:46:26.313 --> 00:46:29.017
MKHAS SO SNYAM DU MI SEMS SO

00:46:29.017 --> 00:46:31.267
I don't think you're so smart.

00:46:31.267 --> 00:46:32.830
[laughing]

00:46:32.830 --> 00:46:35.244
MKHAS SO means...

00:46:35.244 --> 00:46:37.350
MKHAS PA, right Tim?

00:46:37.350 --> 00:46:39.987
Sage, yeah.

00:46:39.987 --> 00:46:44.080
If thou thinkest that
the fire is the wood,

00:46:44.080 --> 00:46:48.130
or if thou thinkest that the
fire and the wood are separate,

00:46:48.130 --> 00:46:52.180
I don't thinketh that thou is too smart.

00:46:52.180 --> 00:46:55.990
And that's his last word
and he's kind of insulting.

00:46:55.990 --> 00:46:57.606
Last word.

00:46:57.606 --> 00:46:59.160
MKHAS SO SNYAM means

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:01.070
if you think you're a smart guy,

00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:04.644
MI SEMS, I don't think so.

00:47:04.644 --> 00:47:07.676
If you don't get the fire-firewood thing,

00:47:07.676 --> 00:47:09.200
then you're a fool.

00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:10.930
That's all. All right.

00:47:10.930 --> 00:47:11.763
And if you're a fool,

00:47:11.763 --> 00:47:14.046
then you're always going to be suffering.

00:47:15.422 --> 00:47:18.765
Somehow, you're always
going to be suffering.

00:47:19.780 --> 00:47:23.390
Now I thought, since
we have a few minutes,

00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:28.543
oh man, my computer just freaked out.

00:47:32.030 --> 00:47:35.520
Oh, yeah.

00:47:35.520 --> 00:47:38.170
You got a picture for me, Pachi?

00:47:38.170 --> 00:47:39.740
I keep wanting to call you Seiji

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:40.912
because your name ends in I.

00:47:40.912 --> 00:47:43.150
[Pachi] Is it the turtle?
[GMR] Yeah.

00:47:43.150 --> 00:47:46.300
I heard Tim likes turtles,
so I got a turtle picture.

00:47:47.342 --> 00:47:49.060
I love this picture.

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:51.600
This is an ancient belief in India

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:55.770
that the whole universe
is held up by a turtle,

00:47:55.770 --> 00:47:58.763
that the universe exists
on the back of a turtle.

00:48:00.710 --> 00:48:05.140
Now listen, if you're
wrong about the turtle

00:48:05.140 --> 00:48:09.120
that holds up the world,
you're wrong about everything

00:48:09.120 --> 00:48:10.530
in the world.

00:48:10.530 --> 00:48:11.530
Got it?

00:48:11.530 --> 00:48:15.573
If you think the world is being
held up on a turtle's back.

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:20.800
- What's being held up on a turtle's back?

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:21.790
- The world.

00:48:21.790 --> 00:48:23.620
- How much stuff is in the world?

00:48:23.620 --> 00:48:25.020
- Everything in the world.

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:25.920
- Okay, then you're wrong

00:48:25.920 --> 00:48:29.773
about every god damn thing
in the world. Okay, got it?

00:48:29.773 --> 00:48:33.620
If you think that world exists
on the back of a turtle,

00:48:33.620 --> 00:48:36.462
then how many things are you wrong about?

00:48:36.462 --> 00:48:37.670
[laughing]

00:48:37.670 --> 00:48:39.520
- Everything on the back.

00:48:39.520 --> 00:48:40.930
- How much is that?

00:48:40.930 --> 00:48:44.473
- That's everything.

00:48:46.280 --> 00:48:48.540
By the way, people are
always bringing me books

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:49.520
all the time.

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:50.403
You don't know.

00:48:51.250 --> 00:48:55.060
And I don't know why
especially when I leave a city

00:48:55.060 --> 00:48:58.010
or a country, they always give
me some book at the airport.

00:49:00.345 --> 00:49:02.400
It's like, "Oh, Gheshe-la, read this,

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:05.075
it's just like what you teach."

00:49:05.075 --> 00:49:06.930
I'm like, I don't think so.

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:09.670
But I'm always polite. I take the book.

00:49:09.670 --> 00:49:12.500
And I have a game I play with myself

00:49:12.500 --> 00:49:15.130
while I'm waiting at the passport line.

00:49:15.130 --> 00:49:18.850
I open the book at one
sentence and I look at it,

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:22.870
and if it's not ridiculous,
I might read another page.

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:25.480
But if the one sentence is ridiculous,

00:49:25.480 --> 00:49:27.620
then when I get through
Customs, and I make sure

00:49:27.620 --> 00:49:29.250
that people aren't looking at me anymore,

00:49:29.250 --> 00:49:30.740
and then I throw it out.

00:49:30.740 --> 00:49:33.397
I throw the book out and
then my friends are like,

00:49:33.397 --> 00:49:37.040
"Geshe-la, why don't you
give it to the charity store

00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:37.887
when you get home?"

00:49:37.887 --> 00:49:41.230
No, I don't want to
because it's totally wrong.

00:49:41.230 --> 00:49:42.500
I don't want to be responsible

00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:46.097
for someone else getting a
stupid idea in their mind

00:49:46.097 --> 00:49:47.930
that's going to cause them suffering

00:49:47.930 --> 00:49:49.210
for the rest of the life.

00:49:49.210 --> 00:49:52.535
No, the only thing I can do
with this book is toss it

00:49:54.050 --> 00:49:56.453
and hope that the garbage
guy doesn't read it.

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:01.390
I'm not being sectarian. I'm just saying,

00:50:01.390 --> 00:50:06.390
if Nagarjuna is correct so
far, then everything we know,

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:09.800
everything that was taught
to me at Princeton University

00:50:09.800 --> 00:50:10.800
was wrong.

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:12.010
Everything.

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:14.183
It was all on the back of a turtle.

00:50:15.340 --> 00:50:18.100
The basic premise was wrong.

00:50:18.100 --> 00:50:19.670
Everything about it was wrong.

00:50:19.670 --> 00:50:21.300
One thing can cause another.

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:22.900
That's wrong.

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:26.220
Okay, then all science, all
philosophy, all music, all art,

00:50:26.220 --> 00:50:28.033
everything is wrong.

00:50:28.033 --> 00:50:29.300
All right, got it?

00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:30.133
Everything.

00:50:30.133 --> 00:50:32.890
If you think that the world
is on the back of a turtle,

00:50:32.890 --> 00:50:35.980
then everything you think
about the world is wrong

00:50:35.980 --> 00:50:38.493
on a basic level, on a basic level.

00:50:39.400 --> 00:50:40.233
All right.

00:50:41.900 --> 00:50:46.167
So don't give me a book unless
it agrees with Nagarjuna

00:50:46.167 --> 00:50:47.980
because I'm not interested.

00:50:47.980 --> 00:50:50.940
All right. And don't say
it's just like what I teach

00:50:50.940 --> 00:50:54.520
because it's not just
like what I'm teaching.

00:50:54.520 --> 00:50:56.043
It really frustrates me.

00:50:56.900 --> 00:50:59.850
All right. We're going to
do a little Sanskrit lesson.

00:51:01.830 --> 00:51:04.100
Don't think this is useless.

00:51:04.100 --> 00:51:07.890
When you become a Buddha,
you speak Sanskrit.

00:51:07.890 --> 00:51:10.463
So come on, learn a few words now.

00:51:11.500 --> 00:51:14.710
It'll get your mind ready for later.

00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:16.080
So apekṣya (apekshya).

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:17.416
Say apekshya.

00:51:17.416 --> 00:51:19.583
[Students] Apekshya. [GMR] Apekshya, yeah.

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:23.905
Now, this is an
interesting word because...

00:51:23.905 --> 00:51:27.738
Christina, when a Sanskrit
word starts with A, it's

00:51:29.070 --> 00:51:30.110
a negative.

00:51:30.110 --> 00:51:32.856
But here it's not.

00:51:32.856 --> 00:51:35.095
And it's very confusing.

00:51:35.095 --> 00:51:38.666
So it's not a-pekshya,

00:51:38.666 --> 00:51:41.357
it's apa-iksha.

00:51:41.357 --> 00:51:43.370
[Christina] Yeah.
[GMR] Yeah, okay, got it?

00:51:43.370 --> 00:51:44.450
So yeah.

00:51:44.450 --> 00:51:48.950
So it's not a-pekshya, like
a-vidya, ignorance, not knowing.

00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:55.044
It's not a-pekshya, it's apa-iksha.

00:51:56.740 --> 00:51:58.950
So don't get confused, all right?

00:51:58.950 --> 00:52:01.173
- What's that mean in Tibetan?

00:52:01.173 --> 00:52:02.230
- It's LTOS PA.

00:52:02.230 --> 00:52:03.420
- What LTOS PA mean?

00:52:03.420 --> 00:52:05.200
- Depend on the wall.

00:52:05.200 --> 00:52:06.780
The ladder depends on the wall.

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:08.470
The fire depends on the wood

00:52:08.470 --> 00:52:10.720
and the wood depends on the fire.

00:52:10.720 --> 00:52:12.640
Dependent origination.

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:15.440
They both depend on your seeds.

00:52:15.440 --> 00:52:18.368
It's not that the fire's
depending on the wood

00:52:18.368 --> 00:52:20.440
and it's not that the
woods depend on the fire,

00:52:20.440 --> 00:52:23.970
they are both depending on
your brain and your seeds.

00:52:23.970 --> 00:52:26.040
Got it?

00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:28.060
So dependence becomes crucial.

00:52:28.060 --> 00:52:30.470
The word apekshya becomes crucial.

00:52:30.470 --> 00:52:32.067
Now, it's so beautiful.

00:52:32.067 --> 00:52:36.437
Apa- means away, and away comes from apa-.

00:52:36.437 --> 00:52:38.445
Iksha means you had pariksha.

00:52:38.445 --> 00:52:40.670
[Student] To see.
[GMR] To see, nice.

00:52:40.670 --> 00:52:43.347
So you're looking to
someone else for help.

00:52:43.347 --> 00:52:47.047
Hey, Christina, I need
some fingernail clippers.

00:52:48.350 --> 00:52:50.780
And I look away from myself to you.

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:53.360
It's called looking away from
yourself to another person

00:52:53.360 --> 00:52:55.343
because you need something.

00:52:56.420 --> 00:53:00.290
I'm depending on Christina
for the fingernail clippers,

00:53:00.290 --> 00:53:02.460
just a few minutes ago.

00:53:02.460 --> 00:53:05.573
So that's called apa-iksha,

00:53:06.800 --> 00:53:09.370
looking over there for help,

00:53:09.370 --> 00:53:11.590
looking for what you need. Hey, Jigme,

00:53:11.590 --> 00:53:13.403
can you give me a pizza?

00:53:13.403 --> 00:53:14.800
I'm depending on you.

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:16.700
Apekshya, apekshya.

00:53:16.700 --> 00:53:19.010
I'm looking away to you.

00:53:19.010 --> 00:53:19.843
Got it?

00:53:19.843 --> 00:53:24.843
Okay, we had iksha already
when we did pariksha.

00:53:25.430 --> 00:53:30.260
Yeah, and then investigate,
looking into something.

00:53:30.260 --> 00:53:32.833
Looking around, digging around.

00:53:34.310 --> 00:53:37.010
Yeah, and don't forget... By the way, Tim,

00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:40.640
you may be interested.
The only one...

00:53:40.640 --> 00:53:42.850
This is the Tibetan word is LTOS PA.

00:53:42.850 --> 00:53:47.134
L-T-O-S, la-ta-tak-ta-naro-to-sa, LTOS

00:53:47.134 --> 00:53:50.022
What's the root of that? For $50.

00:53:50.022 --> 00:53:52.309
Wait, you're looking here.

00:53:54.544 --> 00:53:57.536
LTA BA which means to look.

00:53:57.536 --> 00:53:59.640
So the root of the
Tibetan word is the same

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:02.010
as the root of the Sanskrit word.

00:54:02.010 --> 00:54:05.690
When you say dependent
origination in Tibetan,

00:54:05.690 --> 00:54:08.700
you're saying look to somebody else.

00:54:08.700 --> 00:54:09.610
Got it?

00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:11.253
The same root LT.

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:13.580
la-ta-tak-ta

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:14.790
Okay, cool.

00:54:14.790 --> 00:54:16.963
All right, that's your Sanskrit lesson.

00:54:18.030 --> 00:54:19.700
I'm sorry, that's a tough chapter.

00:54:19.700 --> 00:54:23.810
That's maybe the toughest class
we're going to have, maybe.

00:54:23.810 --> 00:54:26.593
So don't get discouraged.

00:54:26.593 --> 00:54:28.976
Look at it again and again and again.

00:54:30.350 --> 00:54:34.320
I spent, I don't know, almost 30 years

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:36.320
trying to figure this stuff out.

00:54:36.320 --> 00:54:38.170
So don't get frustrated.

00:54:38.170 --> 00:54:40.910
Just keep coming back.
Each time you come back,

00:54:40.910 --> 00:54:42.120
you'll understand a little more.

00:54:42.120 --> 00:54:43.053
I say,

00:54:46.060 --> 00:54:48.150
you can't really do mantras

00:54:48.150 --> 00:54:50.650
if you don't understand this stuff.

00:54:50.650 --> 00:54:53.630
You can, I mean, obviously
anybody can do mantras,

00:54:53.630 --> 00:54:56.320
but if you want them to be powerful,

00:54:56.320 --> 00:54:58.310
you have to understand this stuff.

00:54:58.310 --> 00:55:01.041
All right, see you guys later.

